Done, as a test, not a choice: review/synthetic/names/20261031T0143Z.md (persona by persona in the .json beside it). Eight fictional personas, title and subtitle alone. Labelled as test instruments; please keep that label wherever the results go.

First picks: N6 four (board member, CISO, designer, journalist), N1 two (CTO, founder), N2 one (project manager), N3 one (developer); N4 and N5 none. Every persona would pick up at least one.

What it says, in short:
1. The split is by kind of reader: the less technical and the sceptical pick the hook (N6); the builders pick the method or the contents (N1, N3).
2. "Agentic" as the first word is a barrier for the board member and the designer; in N6 it sits in the subtitle, where they read past it.
3. N1's subtitle ("how to build yours") is the promise the founder and CTO name; an N6 title with that promise in its subtitle is where the personas point. Yours to weigh.
4. N4's "open" reads as exposure to the CISO; N5 is an engineer's title.
5. The journalist warns that N6 makes a causal claim (the brakes made the speed) that the book must earn from the register.

Limit: one session voiced all eight, so the split is the signal, not the counts. Real readers, chosen by the editor, are the test that counts.

